  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nExpect some morning fog across the area. Some areas could see\nsome patchy dense fog. This should begin to clear up by 15Z. The\nFriday period will feature a mainly dry forecast across the \narea with the exception of the region south of I-70. The cold \nfront has settled south of I-70 but still impacting the southern\nOH and northern WV counties. Expect the probabilities of \nrainfall to exceed an a half inch being around 10% to 30%. The \nafternoon convection south of I-70 looks to weaken and dissipate\nby the 21Z to 23Z time frame. Overall, a dry day expected with \nlittle to no impacts. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nExpect more rounds of showers and thunderstorms starting \nSaturday into early next week. This is due to the region \ngenerally sitting on the NE periphery of a lower Mississippi to \nTennessee River Valley high while a series of shortwaves \ntraverse the ridge top. Additionally, warm, moist advection \nfavors a return of the humid airmass characterized by \nanomalously high PWAT values ranging from 1.4\"-1.9\", all of \nwhich is above the 90th percentile. Though finer details vary \nmodel-to-model and day-to-day (some discussed momentarily), the \nbroader messaging of this pattern lends to likely periods \nfeaturing showers/thunderstorm that carry an increased risk for \nflash flooding and, pending other environmental aspects, severe \nweather. The Saturday period will feature the arrival of\nconvection over the northwest portion of the forecast area. The\ntiming seems to be more of a Saturday night threat. \n\nThe threat for flash flooding seems to be in a Saturday night to\nSunday night period. The ensembles have been trending and/or bia\nto lower rainfall totals through this period and is very\nnoticeable. This is true even for the NBM. Thus, will expect an\nMCS to track across the area in the late Saturday night period.  \nCurrent ensemble mean rainfall total values over this 24-hour \nperiod range from 0.75\"-1.5\" with the axis favoring the I-80 \ncorridor. Pending the potential north/south re- positioning of \nthis axis, high-end scenarios could see totals in the heaviest \naxis in the 2-3\" range; any additional convective element boost \ntheoretically could push that higher. Entering this period, the \nregion already is fairly saturated with lowered flash flood \nguidance; any rain that develops AFTER this initial window \nlikely does with almost fully saturated grounds that sees a high\npercentage of rainfall translate to run-off (increasing flash \nflood likelihood). If confidence in these outcomes increase, \nFlood Watches may be issued in the coming days.",
